  "theoryHtml": "<section class=\"theory-didactica\" lang=\"en\"><h2>What is it and why?</h2><p>Laboratory English helps you name tools, actions, science areas and jobs used in Madame Curie Science class.</p><h2>Key rules</h2><ul><li>Learn the English term and its meaning.</li><li>Listen to the pronunciation and repeat aloud.</li><li>Group words by family: verbs, containers, measuring tools, safety gear, -y sciences, -ist jobs.</li></ul><h2>Good vs bad</h2><ul><li><strong>Good:</strong> beaker = glass container for liquids</li><li><strong>Bad:</strong> calling a beaker a “cup” in a lab report</li></ul><h2>Teacher tip</h2><p>If it ends in -y it is often a science area (Biology). If it ends in -ist it is often a person (Biologist).</p><h2>1-minute summary</h2><p>Flashcards → memory → quiz → spelling → sorter. Practise until the 35 lab words feel natural.</p></section>",
